  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: A rather flat southwest to northeast oriented\nflow regime is in place to start the forecast period. An impulse\nembedded within this flow will track mainly across the northern\ntier of states/southern Canada, which will drag a cold front\nthrough the Upper Great Lakes region later tonight into early\nThursday.\n\nBehind the above mentioned disturbance, heights generally build\nacross much of eastern North America into early next week. A\nstronger, slow moving, potentially cutoff trof will approach the\nGreat Lakes region after Monday, but model agreement in the\ndetails with this system is not very good right now. There is\nstill plenty of time to get the details with this system ironed\nout.\n\n\nForecast Details: Very mild conditions for late October linger\ninto tonight ahead of an approaching cold front. Scattered showers\nand possibly a thunderstorm are expected along/ahead of the \nfront after midnight tonight into early Thursday. The convection\nwill be elevated with a southwesterly low-level jet as high as \n50 kts. Mid-level lapse rates are fairly steep as well ahead of \nthe front so some of the showers may be robust where they occur.\nThe weakening of the nocturnal low-level jet should result in \ndiminishing coverage/intensity of precipitation with eastward \nextent.\n\nNot a whole lot of weather of note through the weekend in the\nwake of the front. Temperatures cool behind the front, although\nstill a little warmer than normal, but warm again after Friday\nas upper-level heights rise and southerly low-level flow\nreturns. Rain chances start to return Monday night as a\nstronger, but currently low confidence system approaches.",
